{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1787138060,"nonce":"DX796MiFBsFN40uLdvxCat/ProQ=","message":"⛔ **OWNER RULING, 2026-08-20: `mention_on_reminder` GOES WITH IT. Question 1 is closed.**\n\nAsked directly — *\"when we are dropping the entire reminder flow what's the point of `mention_on_reminder`\"* — and the answer is that there isn't one. A mention is not a property of any Slack delivery in oto; it is the audience half of the one reminder, and it exists nowhere else. With no reminder there is nobody to address.\n\nSo the three mention keys go with the fourth key, not after it:\n\n- `unacked_reminder_mention` (`internal/identity/domain/settings.go:53`)\n- `unacked_reminder_mention_list` (`:55`)\n- `unacked_reminder_mention_min_severity` (`:59`)\n\nplus their resolved fields (`:313-323`), `internal/identity/domain/mention.go`, the `internal/identity/repository/orgs.go:59-61` reads, the tuning-screen copy, the Helm values, the contract and the regenerated client — and, because it is now unreachable rather than merely unconfigured, the Slack render path and its checked-in goldens: `internal/channels/render/slack/reply.go`, `testdata/reply_unacked_reminder_mention.golden.json`, `test/fixtures/slack/broadcast_unacked_reminder.message.json` and its `index.json` entry.\n\n⭐ **This makes the SCOPE-BOUNDARY supersession cleaner, not messier, and that is worth noticing before anyone writes it.** `§5.2` restricted `mention_on_reminder` to usergroups and `!here`/`!channel` and dropped individual `\u003c@U…\u003e` mentions, calling that restriction \"the ruling\" (`:384`). The restriction existed to stop oto naming a responder — H-1, \"oto does not know who is on call and must never learn\". Removing the whole mention surface does not weaken that boundary; it removes the only place oto could have crossed it. The supersession note should say so, rather than reading as a retreat.\n\n⚠️ **Question 2 is still open** and is now the only thing blocking this ticket: what happens to the §G.9 binding clause (\"there is exactly ONE stage, forever... a second stage is an escalation policy and is permanently OUT\"), and to `escalation` on the §A.1 banned-word list. My read, offered as a starting point rather than a decision: the banned word **stays** — it was banned because escalation vocabulary drags in rotas and ownership, which is true whether or not oto reminds anyone — while the one-stage clause becomes a statement that oto sends no unprompted reminders at all, which is a *stronger* boundary than the one it replaces. That still needs the owner to say it.","files":null}]}